<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
  <meta name="description" content="Eight compact house-price baselines and models compared on chronological Ames Housing validation and a 2010 holdout.">
  <link rel="icon" href="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 64 64'%3E%3Crect width='64' height='64' rx='14' fill='%23122840'/%3E%3Cpath d='M12 32 32 14 52 32V52H37V38H27V52H12Z' fill='%237fdfcc'/%3E%3C/svg%3E">
  <title>Ames Housing · Model benchmark</title>
  <style>
    :root{--ink:#213547;--muted:#64748b;--line:#dce4ed;--blue:#335fe5;--teal:#087f75;--bg:#f3f6fa;--navy:#122840}
    *{box-sizing:border-box}html{scroll-behavior:smooth}body{margin:0;color:var(--ink);background:var(--bg);font:15px/1.65 system-ui,-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,"Segoe UI",sans-serif}a{color:var(--blue);text-underline-offset:3px}button,select{font:inherit}button{cursor:pointer}button:focus-visible,a:focus-visible,select:focus-visible,summary:focus-visible{outline:3px solid #95b5ff;outline-offset:4px}.wrap{max-width:1260px;margin:auto;padding:0 30px}
    header{background:var(--navy);color:#fff;padding:46px 0 42px;border-bottom:5px solid var(--teal)}.topline{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:20px;align-items:center;margin-bottom:30px}.eyebrow{font-size:12px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.15em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#b4c8e5}.status{border:1px solid #537083;padding:5px 12px;border-radius:30px;font-size:12px;color:#cfe9e3}h1{font-size:clamp(34px,4.7vw,57px);line-height:1.1;letter-spacing:-.045em;margin:0 0 20px;max-width:900px}header p{max-width:820px;color:#c7d5e6;font-size:17px;margin:0}.header-meta{margin-top:24px;font-size:12px;color:#9eb2cd}.accent{color:#7fdfcc}
    nav{background:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)}nav .wrap{display:flex;gap:24px;padding-top:14px;padding-bottom:14px;flex-wrap:wrap}nav a{font-size:13px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:650;color:#486079}main{padding-top:28px!important;padding-bottom:50px!important}section{scroll-margin-top:20px;margin-bottom:25px}.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:13px;padding:28px;margin-bottom:24px}.section-head{display:flex;align-items:baseline;justify-content:space-between;gap:15px;flex-wrap:wrap}.kicker{font-size:11px;letter-spacing:.13em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--teal);font-weight:750;margin:0 0 8px}h2{font-size:25px;letter-spacing:-.025em;line-height:1.3;margin:0 0 12px}h3{font-size:17px;line-height:1.4;margin:0 0 9px}p{margin:0 0 14px}.muted,.caption{color:var(--muted)}.caption{font-size:12px;margin:8px 0 0}.small{font-size:13px}.grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:24px}.metrics{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);gap:14px;margin:24px 0}.metric{background:#f6f9fc;border:1px solid #e2e9f1;border-radius:9px;padding:18px}.metric strong{display:block;letter-spacing:-.04em;font-size:31px;line-height:1.25;color:var(--navy)}.metric span{display:block;color:var(--muted);font-size:12px;margin-top:6px}.recommend{border-top:4px solid var(--teal)}.recommendation{font-size:19px;max-width:1020px}.pill{display:inline-block;border-radius:20px;padding:2px 9px;font-size:11px;font-weight:650;white-space:nowrap;background:#edf2ff;color:#39589d}.pill.green{background:#e3f4ef;color:#086559}.note{padding:15px 18px;border-left:3px solid var(--blue);background:#f0f5ff;border-radius:0 6px 6px 0;margin:18px 0;font-size:13px}.note.warm{border-color:#c18427;background:#fff7e9}.split{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(5,1fr);gap:8px;margin:20px 0}.year{padding:16px 12px;background:#edf3fb;border-radius:7px;text-align:center}.year.test{background:#e1f3ee}.year strong{font-size:20px;display:block}.year span{font-size:12px;color:#516581}.chart{min-width:0;width:100%}.chart .plotly-graph-div{width:100%!important}.table-scroll{overflow-x:auto;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;margin:16px 0}table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13px}th,td{padding:12px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid #e6edf4;text-align:left;vertical-align:top}th{background:#eff4f9;color:#4a5d73;font-size:11px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.025em;white-space:nowrap}td.num,th.num{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;white-space:nowrap}tbody tr:last-child td{border-bottom:0}tbody tr:nth-child(even){background:#fafbfd}tr.selected{background:#eef8f4!important}td.model{min-width:180px}td.model strong{display:block}td.model small{display:block;font-size:11px;color:var(--muted);margin-top:4px}.toolbar{display:flex;gap:9px;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center}.button{border:1px solid #c7d4e3;border-radius:6px;background:#fff;color:#385271;padding:8px 13px;font-size:12px;font-weight:650;text-decoration:none}.button:hover{background:#eef4ff}.selector{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;font-size:12px;margin:14px 0}select{border:1px solid #c2cfdf;border-radius:5px;padding:7px;background:white;color:var(--ink)}details{border-top:1px solid var(--line);padding:15px 0}summary{cursor:pointer;font-weight:650;color:#345a8c}details p{margin:12px 0;font-size:13px}pre{background:#142b43;color:#e3edf7;border-radius:7px;padding:17px;overflow-x:auto;font:12px/1.7 ui-monospace,monospace}code{font:12px/1.6 ui-monospace,monospace;background:#edf2f8;padding:2px 5px;border-radius:4px}pre code{background:none;padding:0;color:inherit}.model-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);gap:0 25px}.definition{border-top:1px solid var(--line);padding-top:12px;margin-top:12px}.definition strong{display:block;font-size:14px}.definition p{font-size:13px;color:var(--muted);margin-top:4px}.number{font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.nowrap{white-space:nowrap}footer{font-size:12px;color:var(--muted);padding:0 3px}.visually-hidden{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0,0,0,0)}
    @media(max-width:800px){.wrap{padding:0 16px}.card{padding:22px 16px}.grid,.model-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.metrics{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr)}.topline{align-items:start}.status{max-width:150px;text-align:center}nav .wrap{gap:15px}.split{gap:5px}.year{padding:10px 3px}.year span{font-size:10px}.year strong{font-size:17px}.metric strong{font-size:27px}h1{font-size:36px}.chart{overflow-x:auto}.chart .plotly-graph-div{min-width:620px}}
    @media print{body{background:white;font-size:10pt}.wrap{padding:0;max-width:none}header{background:white;color:var(--ink);padding:10px 0;border-color:var(--line)}header p,.header-meta,.eyebrow{color:var(--muted)}h1{font-size:30px}.accent{color:var(--teal)}nav,.toolbar,.selector,.status,.modebar{display:none!important}.card{border:0;padding:15px 0;margin:0}.chart,.metrics{break-inside:avoid}.table-scroll{overflow:visible}.grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}details{break-inside:avoid}a{color:inherit}}
    @media(max-width:800px){.grid,.model-grid{grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr)}.grid>*,.model-grid>*{min-width:0}}
  </style>
  <script>{{ plotly_js | safe }}</script>
</head>
<body>
<header><div class="wrap">
  <div class="topline"><span class="eyebrow">Ames Housing / Model lab</span><span class="status">Completed · {{ metadata.elapsed_seconds | decimal }} seconds*</span></div>
  <h1>Which model predicts<br> <span class="accent">house prices best?</span></h1>
  <p>Seven compact algorithms, one simple baseline, and a test on later sales. Compare prediction errors, training speed, and the tradeoffs behind the numbers.</p>
  <div class="header-meta">{{ rows | integer }} sales · Ames, Iowa · Historical USD, 2006–2010 · Generated {{ generated }}</div>
</div></header>
<nav aria-label="Report navigation"><div class="wrap"><a href="#result">Recommendation</a>{% if market %}<a href="#market-2026">2026 scenarios</a>{% endif %}<a href="#leaderboard">Model comparison</a><a href="#diagnostics">Errors</a><a href="#speed">Speed & size</a><a href="#method">Method</a><a href="#glossary">KPI guide</a><a href="#reproduce">Use the model</a></div></nav>
<main class="wrap">
  <section class="card recommend" id="result">
    <p class="kicker">Selected before the test was scored</p>
    <h2>{{ winner.name }}</h2>
    <p class="recommendation">The lowest average validation RMSE across 2007, 2008, and 2009: <strong>{{ winner.cv_rmse | money }}</strong>. On {{ test_rows }} later sales in 2010, its average absolute error was <strong>{{ winner.test.mae | money }}</strong>, with <strong>{{ winner.test.within_10 | percent }}</strong> of predictions within 10% of the sale price.</p>
    <div class="metrics">
      <div class="metric"><strong>{{ winner.test.rmse | money }}</strong><span>Test RMSE · lower is better</span></div>
      <div class="metric"><strong>{{ winner.test.mae | money }}</strong><span>Test MAE · average dollar error</span></div>
      <div class="metric"><strong>{{ winner.test.r2 | decimal }}</strong><span>Test R² · closer to 1 is better</span></div>
      <div class="metric"><strong>{{ winner.fit_seconds | decimal }}s</strong><span>Final fit on {{ dev_rows | integer }} homes</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="grid"><div><h3>{{ baseline_improvement | percent }} lower RMSE than the baseline</h3><p class="small muted">The baseline always estimates a training-set median price. Its test RMSE was {{ baseline.test.rmse | money }}, compared with {{ winner.test.rmse | money }} for {{ winner.name }}.</p></div><div><h3>A lead of {{ cv_gap | money }} over {{ runner_up }}</h3><p class="small muted">This is the difference in average validation RMSE. Three validation years establish a useful comparison, but do not prove one algorithm always wins. {% if test_best != winner.name %}{{ test_best }} had the lowest 2010 test RMSE; the saved recommendation remains the model selected on validation.{% else %}The validation choice also had the lowest 2010 test RMSE.{% endif %}</p></div></div>
    <div class="note warm">These are estimates of 2006–2010 Ames prices. The model has not been validated for today’s market or another city. The earlier EDA viewed the whole dataset, so 2010 is reserved for this training procedure, but is not a fully unseen external benchmark.</div>
  </section>

  {% if market %}{% include "ames_market_section.html.j2" %}{% endif %}
  <section class="card" id="leaderboard">
    <div class="section-head"><div><p class="kicker">01 / Accuracy</p><h2>The model comparison</h2></div><div class="toolbar"><button class="button" data-download="metrics">Download KPI CSV</button><button class="button" data-download="validation">Download validation CSV</button></div></div>
    <p class="muted">Rows are ordered by average validation RMSE, which selected the saved model. All models use the same properties, folds, and predictors. The 2010 test columns provide a final check.</p>
    <div class="chart">{{ charts.comparison | safe }}</div>
    <p class="caption">On narrow screens, swipe charts and tables horizontally to see every model.</p>
    <label class="selector">Order table by <select id="sort-models"><option value="rank">Validation rank (selection rule)</option><option value="rmse">2010 test RMSE</option><option value="mae">2010 test MAE</option><option value="speed">Total fitting time</option></select></label>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table id="model-table"><caption class="visually-hidden">Validation and test metrics for every model</caption><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th class="num">Validation<br>RMSE ± year SD</th><th class="num">Test RMSE ↓</th><th class="num">Test MAE ↓</th><th class="num">Test R² ↑</th><th class="num">Test MAPE ↓</th><th class="num">Within 10% ↑</th><th class="num">All fits ↓</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    {% for r in records %}<tr class="{{ 'selected' if r.name == winner.name else '' }}" data-rank="{{ r.rank }}" data-rmse="{{ r.test.rmse }}" data-mae="{{ r.test.mae }}" data-speed="{{ r.total_fit_seconds }}"><td class="model"><strong>{{ r.name }}</strong><small>{{ r.family }}{% if r.name == winner.name %} · <span class="pill green">Saved model</span>{% elif r.name == 'Median baseline' %} · Reference only{% else %} · Validation #{{ r.rank }}{% endif %}</small></td><td class="num">{{ r.cv_rmse | money }}<br><span class="muted">± {{ r.cv_rmse_std | money }}</span></td><td class="num">{{ r.test.rmse | money }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.test.mae | money }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.test.r2 | decimal }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.test.mape | percent }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.test.within_10 | percent }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.total_fit_seconds | decimal }}s</td></tr>{% endfor %}
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="caption">Dollar metrics are calculated after converting predictions back from log prices. Year SD measures variation across three validation years, not a confidence interval. MAPE can be distorted by very low sale prices. “All fits” includes three validation fits and one final fit.</p>
    <details><summary>How stable was each model across years?</summary><div class="chart">{{ charts.stability | safe }}</div><p class="caption">Click a legend label to show or hide a model. All seven algorithms and the baseline are included; four curves are initially visible. Each validation year contains different properties and training-set sizes.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th class="num">2007 RMSE</th><th class="num">2008 RMSE</th><th class="num">2009 RMSE</th></tr></thead><tbody>{% for r in records %}<tr><td>{{ r.name }}</td>{% for fold in r.folds %}<td class="num">{{ fold.rmse | money }}</td>{% endfor %}</tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table></div></details>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="diagnostics">
    <div class="section-head"><div><p class="kicker">02 / A closer look</p><h2>Where {{ winner.name }} makes mistakes</h2></div><button class="button" data-download="predictions">Download all test predictions</button></div>
    <div class="grid"><div><h3>Predicted versus actual price</h3><div class="chart">{{ charts.scatter | safe }}</div><p class="caption">Every dot is one 2010 sale. Predictions on the dashed diagonal are exact. Hover for dollar values.</p></div><div><h3>Does error grow with price?</h3><div class="chart">{{ charts.residual | safe }}</div><p class="caption">Above zero = overestimate; below zero = underestimate. Large errors count more heavily in RMSE.</p></div></div>
    <div class="metrics"><div class="metric"><strong>{{ winner.test.median_ape | percent }}</strong><span>Median absolute percentage error</span></div><div class="metric"><strong>{{ winner.test.within_20 | percent }}</strong><span>Predictions within ±20%</span></div><div class="metric"><strong>{{ winner.test.bias | money }}</strong><span>Mean bias · prediction − actual</span></div><div class="metric"><strong>{{ test_rows }}</strong><span>Test properties · all 2010 rows</span></div></div>
    <h3>Performance by actual sale price</h3><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Price segment</th><th class="num">Homes</th><th class="num">MAE ↓</th><th class="num">RMSE ↓</th><th class="num">Median APE ↓</th><th class="num">Within 10% ↑</th><th class="num">Bias</th></tr></thead><tbody>{% for s in segments %}<tr><td>{{ s.label }}</td><td class="num">{{ s.count }}</td><td class="num">{{ s.mae | money }}</td><td class="num">{{ s.rmse | money }}</td><td class="num">{{ s.median_ape | percent }}</td><td class="num">{{ s.within_10 | percent }}</td><td class="num">{{ s.bias | money }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table></div>
    <p class="small muted">Approximate 95% bootstrap intervals: MAE <strong>{{ ci.mae[0] | money }}–{{ ci.mae[1] | money }}</strong>; RMSE <strong>{{ ci.rmse[0] | money }}–{{ ci.rmse[1] | money }}</strong>. Based on 1,500 resamples of the test properties, treating rows as independent. These describe sampling uncertainty for these test errors, exclude model-selection and market-change uncertainty, and are not per-house prediction intervals.</p>
    <details><summary>The 12 largest dollar errors</summary><p class="muted">Unusual homes and sale circumstances remain in the test set. These cases are shown for diagnosis; no model was changed after seeing them.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Source row</th><th>Neighborhood</th><th class="num">Living area (sq ft)</th><th class="num">Actual</th><th class="num">Predicted</th><th class="num">Absolute error</th><th class="num">APE</th></tr></thead><tbody>{% for w in worst %}<tr><td>{{ w.Order }}</td><td>{{ w.Neighborhood }}</td><td class="num">{{ w['Gr Liv Area'] | integer }}</td><td class="num">{{ w.SalePrice | money }}</td><td class="num">{{ w[winner.name] | money }}</td><td class="num">{{ w.selected_absolute_error_usd | money }}</td><td class="num">{{ w.selected_ape_pct | percent }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table></div></details>
    <details><summary>Training errors and the generalization gap</summary><p class="muted">A much lower training error can signal overfitting. Training and test properties also differ in year and price mix; the gap alone cannot isolate the cause.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th class="num">Training RMSE</th><th class="num">Test RMSE</th><th class="num">Difference</th></tr></thead><tbody>{% for r in records %}<tr><td>{{ r.name }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.train.rmse | money }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.test.rmse | money }}</td><td class="num">{{ (r.test.rmse-r.train.rmse) | money }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table></div></details>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="speed">
    <p class="kicker">03 / Practical cost</p><h2>Small enough to run locally</h2><p class="muted">Fixed configurations, two CPU threads, and no parameter search. Timing depends on this workstation and its current load.</p>
    <div class="chart">{{ charts.speed | safe }}</div>
    <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Size limit</th><th class="num">Final fit</th><th class="num">Predict 1,000*</th></tr></thead><tbody>{% for r in records %}<tr><td class="nowrap">{{ r.name }}</td><td>{{ r.size }}{% if r.epochs is defined %}<br><strong>Actual: {{ r.epochs }} epochs, {{ r.network_parameters | integer }} weights and biases.</strong>{% endif %}</td><td class="num">{{ r.fit_seconds | decimal }}s</td><td class="num">{{ r.predict_ms_per_1000 | decimal }} ms</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table></div>
    <p class="caption">*Prediction timing is the median of five warmed batches of {{ test_rows }} homes, normalized to 1,000. Includes preprocessing; does not include loading the saved file. End-to-end analysis time in the header includes feature importance, scoring and model serialization; it excludes HTML rendering. All models have {{ winner.encoded_features }} encoded features in their final training fit.</p>
    <details><summary>What each algorithm does and its exact configuration</summary><div class="model-grid">{% for r in records %}<article class="definition"><h3>{{ r.name }}</h3><p>{{ r.explanation }}</p><p>{{ r.size }}</p><pre>{{ r.parameters | tojson(indent=2) }}</pre>{% if r.warnings %}<p><strong>Training notices:</strong> {{ r.warnings | join('; ') }}</p>{% endif %}</article>{% endfor %}</div></details>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="method">
    <p class="kicker">04 / How this was measured</p><h2>Earlier homes train the model. Later homes test it.</h2>
    <div class="split"><div class="year"><strong>2006</strong><span>625 sales · development</span></div><div class="year"><strong>2007</strong><span>694 sales · development</span></div><div class="year"><strong>2008</strong><span>622 sales · development</span></div><div class="year"><strong>2009</strong><span>648 sales · development</span></div><div class="year test"><strong>2010</strong><span>{{ test_rows }} sales · final test</span></div></div>
    <div class="grid"><div><h3>1. Compare on three forward splits</h3><p class="small muted">Train on earlier years and validate on the next calendar year. Choose the lowest mean RMSE, giving each validation year equal weight. Hyperparameters are fixed in advance; there is no exhaustive tuning.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Train years</th><th class="num">Homes</th><th>Validate</th><th class="num">Homes</th></tr></thead><tbody>{% for f in metadata.folds %}<tr><td>{{ f.train_years }}</td><td class="num">{{ f.train_rows }}</td><td>{{ f.validation_year }}</td><td class="num">{{ f.validation_rows }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody></table></div></div><div><h3>2. Refit, then score 2010 once</h3><p class="small muted">Each model is refit on all {{ dev_rows | integer }} sales from 2006–2009, then evaluated on the same {{ test_rows }} sales from 2010. The winner is selected before these test scores are computed. The saved pipeline is this evaluated 2006–2009 fit.</p><h3>3. Keep every property</h3><p class="small muted">No outliers or unusual sales are removed. Every record belongs to development or test. Parcel IDs are unique, so no parcel appears in both groups. Temporal validation tests later Ames sales, rather than a random sample of the same years.</p></div></div>
    <details open><summary>Inputs and preprocessing</summary><div class="grid"><div><p><strong>{{ feature_count }} property and calendar inputs:</strong> {{ numeric_count }} numeric fields and {{ categorical_count }} categorical fields. <code>Order</code> and <code>PID</code> are excluded identifiers; <code>Sale Type</code> and <code>Sale Condition</code> are excluded because they may be unknown before a sale. The target <code>SalePrice</code> is never an input.</p><p>Recorded sale month and year are predictors; a future estimate therefore assumes the intended sale calendar is known. Other property attributes are assumed available at valuation time. That availability has not been independently audited.</p></div><div><p><strong>Learned separately inside each training fit:</strong> numeric median imputation, indicators for numeric missingness, numeric standardization, constant categorical imputation, and one-hot category encoding. Categories appearing fewer than 10 times in training are grouped; the first encoded level is dropped. Previously unseen levels use the infrequent group when available, otherwise all-zero encoding.</p><p>Documented literal <code>NA</code> categories (for example, no pool) are preserved; only blank cells are treated as missing. Nominal numeric codes such as <code>MS SubClass</code> stay categorical. Source files are unchanged.</p></div></div><p>All seven learned models fit <code>log(1 + SalePrice)</code> and convert predictions back with <code>expm1</code>. This reduces the influence of expensive homes during training and emphasizes relative differences; predictions have no mean-bias correction. The neural network also standardizes this log target and reserves 15% of its own training rows for early stopping. The median baseline directly predicts USD. All reported KPIs are in the original price scale.</p>{% if metadata.test_unknown_category_counts %}<p><strong>Unseen categories in the 2010 test:</strong> {% for name, count in metadata.test_unknown_category_counts.items() %}{{ name }} ({{ count }} rows){{ ', ' if not loop.last else '.' }}{% endfor %}</p>{% endif %}</details>
    <details><summary>Which features helped the selected model?</summary><p>Permutation importance on the 2009 validation set, using the selected model trained on 2006–2008. Each original feature is shuffled three times; the chart shows the increase in dollar RMSE, with one standard deviation across shuffles. The test set is not used to compute this chart.</p><div class="chart">{{ charts.importance | safe }}</div><p class="caption">Importance describes this fitted model. Correlated features can share or hide importance. It does not show that changing a feature would cause a price change.</p></details>
    <div class="note">This is a compact first benchmark. Different size limits, features, or tuning could change the ranking. The 2010 period is only part of a calendar year, represents one city, and contains {{ test_rows }} homes. Review the error segments before relying on an individual estimate.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="card" id="glossary"><p class="kicker">05 / Understand the numbers</p><h2>A practical KPI guide</h2><div class="grid"><div><div class="definition"><strong>MAE · Mean absolute error ↓</strong><p>Average distance between a prediction and the true sale price, in dollars. An MAE of {{ winner.test.mae | money }} means the absolute errors average that amount; individual errors can be much larger.</p></div><div class="definition"><strong>RMSE · Root mean squared error ↓</strong><p>Square each dollar error, average, then take the square root. Gives more weight to large misses. This benchmark’s model-selection KPI.</p></div><div class="definition"><strong>R² · Explained variation ↑</strong><p>Compares squared errors with predicting the mean of the evaluated sales. 1 is perfect, 0 matches that reference, and negative is worse. R² is not a percentage of correctly priced homes.</p></div><div class="definition"><strong>MAPE & median APE ↓</strong><p>Absolute error divided by actual price, expressed as a percentage. MAPE averages these values; median APE is their midpoint. Low-priced homes can have disproportionately large percentage errors.</p></div></div><div><div class="definition"><strong>Within 10% / 20% ↑</strong><p>Share of test predictions within ±10% or ±20% of actual price. Easy to interpret as a tolerance-based success rate.</p></div><div class="definition"><strong>Bias · Near zero is better</strong><p>Average prediction minus actual price. Positive means overpricing on average; negative means underpricing. Overestimates and underestimates can cancel, so bias never replaces MAE.</p></div><div class="definition"><strong>Validation year SD</strong><p>Sample standard deviation of RMSE across the three validation years. A larger value means the model’s observed performance varies more across these years.</p></div><div class="definition"><strong>Fit and prediction time ↓</strong><p>Time spent learning, and time applying the learned pipeline. Compare accuracy with the cost of training and using a model.</p></div></div></div></section>

  <section class="card" id="reproduce"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="kicker">06 / Reproduce & use</p><h2>The report comes with a working model</h2></div><div class="toolbar"><button class="button" id="print-report">Print / save PDF</button></div></div>
    <p>The saved model is <strong>{{ winner.name }}</strong>, trained on 2006–2009. Its preprocessing is bundled in the pipeline. Run these commands from the project folder:</p>
    <pre><code>source ~/myenv/bin/activate
# Re-run all comparisons and rebuild this report:
python scripts/train_models.py

# Estimate prices for the three supplied example properties:
python scripts/predict_prices.py \
  --input models/ames/example_properties.csv \
  --output reports/example_predictions.csv</code></pre>
    <p class="small muted">For new input, use the original Ames column names and include all {{ feature_count }} predictor columns; individual cells may be blank. Extra columns are preserved in prediction output. The starter CSV with five inputs is not compatible with this full-feature model. The example properties are test rows provided for demonstration.</p>
    <details><summary>Saved files and reproducibility details</summary><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>scripts/train_models.py</code></td><td>Training, validation, evaluation, charts, and HTML generation</td></tr><tr><td><code>scripts/predict_prices.py</code></td><td>Use the saved pipeline on a CSV of properties</td></tr><tr><td><code>models/ames/best_model.joblib</code></td><td>Fitted model and preprocessing</td></tr><tr><td><code>models/ames/model_metadata.json</code></td><td>Winner, exact metrics, settings, input schema, splits, versions, and data checksum</td></tr><tr><td><code>models/ames/model_metrics.csv</code></td><td>Machine-readable KPI comparison</td></tr><tr><td><code>models/ames/validation_metrics.csv</code></td><td>Every model’s metrics in each validation year</td></tr><tr><td><code>models/ames/test_predictions.csv</code></td><td>Actual values and every model’s predictions for all 2010 homes</td></tr><tr><td><code>models/ames/example_properties.csv</code></td><td>Three example input rows without sale prices</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Random seed:</strong> {{ metadata.random_seed }} · <strong>CPU thread limit:</strong> 2 · <strong>Input SHA-256:</strong> <code style="word-break:break-all">{{ metadata.input_sha256 }}</code></p><p><strong>Package versions:</strong> {% for name, version in metadata.versions.items() %}{{ name }} {{ version }}{{ ' · ' if not loop.last else '' }}{% endfor %}</p><p>Install dependencies with <code>python -m pip install -r requirements-models.txt</code> in the activated environment. Retraining can produce small timing and numerical differences across machines.</p></details>
    <p class="small muted">This HTML embeds its charts and downloadable CSVs. It works offline and can be shared as a single file. Keep the project’s scripts and saved model if you want to train again or make predictions.</p>
  </section>
